{"paper_id": "2605.30497v1", "chunk_id": "arxiv:2605.30497v1:28", "title": "CanLegalRAGBench: Evaluating Retrieval-Augmented Generation on Canadian Case Law", "page_start": 8.0, "page_end": 8.0, "text": "the ungrounded claims were hallucinations. Extrapolating this to results in Tab 6, estimate a lower bound of 8% hallucination rate with an upper bound of 29%. Generated answers differ substantially from the gold standard, especially when based on differ- ent documents. The accuracy of generated an- swers in the oracle condition is 64-76%, demon- strating substantial deviation. We analyze unsup- ported claims in Appendix E.2. The accuracy is much lower in the pipeline condition, when it is based on different documents than the gold stan- dard answer. These findings illustrate the impor- tance of a good retrieval component. 5.4 Analysis Table 5 shows that subgroup trends in generation do follow retrieval trends. Legal-expert queries have higher nDCG@10 for all three family-matched pipelines,"}
{"paper_id": "2605.30497v1", "chunk_id": "arxiv:2605.30497v1:29", "title": "CanLegalRAGBench: Evaluating Retrieval-Augmented Generation on Canadian Case Law", "page_start": 8.0, "page_end": 8.0, "text": "(0.723 and 0.445), despite similar nDCG@10. Thus, legal phrasing helps retrieval ranking, but improvements on generation quality remains to be seen. Our pre- vious categorization of groundedness errors shows there can be a lot of noise in judging. Jurisdictional results show a similar decoupling. Gemini is most accurate and grounded on the \u2018Other\u2019 subset of provinces, and performs worst on Ontario for accuracy (0.284), even with strong retrieval scores. Gemma is weakest on British Columbia across retrieval and generation, and strongest on Alberta for generation. Qwen is more stable across jurisdictions in generation. Across each configuration, there is high deviation across performance on different jurisdictions which high- lights the need for more robust systems or individ- ual systems ta"}
