{"paper_id": "2605.30521v1", "chunk_id": "arxiv:2605.30521v1:25", "title": "Evaluating using Mock Tool Calls to Quarantine Untrusted Prompt Inputs", "page_start": 6.0, "page_end": 6.0, "text": "for unsafe outputs. Complement- ing these attack-side results, Ye et al. [2026] probe how models internally represent \u201cwho is speaking\u201d and find that role perception is driven by the style of the text rather than by the role tag enclosing it. In their exper- iments, user-style content wrapped in tool tags retains 76\u201388% \u201cUserness\u201d across four frontier-class models, with \u201cToolness\u201d staying under 20%. These results help predict the asymmetry we observe. The tool channel is not consistently treated as less-trusted in practice, and in some regimes may be treated as more authoritative. Quarantining-style defenses. Several proposals share the conceptual move of structurally separating untrusted data from trusted instructions, in response to direct and indirect prompt-injection threats [Toyer et "}
